Q: How can I modify the color of the TitleBar in my Windows .NET MAUI 9.0 application? A: <p><code>Shell.BackgroundColor</code> only affects the Shell navigation bar</p>
<p>Configure the Windows title bar on the Window, not inside MainPage</p>
<pre><code>protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? state)
{
    var window = new Window(new AppShell());

    window.TitleBar = new TitleBar
    {
        Title = &quot;My App&quot;,
        BackgroundColor = Colors.SlateGray
    };

    return window;
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>VisualElement.Window</code> is read-only, which is why assigning it inside MainPage causes CS0200.</p>
<p>To hide it: <code>window.TitleBar.IsVisible = false;</code></p>

Q: How to notify listeners of A from B A: <p>Actually, using BuildContext as a parameter for your domain methods are not a good idea at all. It becomes impossible to test it as you cannot mock a BuildContext object and even if you do use the BuildContext inside your methods, you also need to check if the context is mounted in the widget tree, i.e., the widget is still present in the widget tree or not.</p>
<p>To solve this, you can use service locator or any way to provide dependency injection. I have used <a href="https://flutter-it.dev/documentation/get_it/getting_started" rel="nofollow noreferrer">get_It</a> service locator package for this situation. It completely detaches dependencies from your widget tree and the objects can be accessible anywhere in the app without compromising testability.</p>
<p>The way to make changes from one notifier class in another is correct the way you are doing but you just need to change the implementation of object access.</p>
<p>So, here's an example of how it would look with get_It package for Controller B class.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>class GameController extends ChangeNotifier {

  DrawController drawController = DrawController();

  void someFunction(BuildContext context){

     getIt&lt;DrawController&gt;().clearCanvas();//No dependency of BuildContext
     notifyListeners();
   }

}
</code></pre>
